data.content:
## Migration Analysis

Your code has these Thread.Abort patterns:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| **ThreadAbortException catch** | `catch` block | `OperationCanceledException` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort** | catch body | Remove — not needed with cooperative model |
| **Thread.Abort()** | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| **Blocking call** | `GetNextItem()` | Pass token so it can unblock |

## Key Pitfalls for This Code

1. **`GetNextItem()` blocks** — `Cancel()` won't interrupt it unless you pass the token through. If it does synchronous I/O, switch to an async overload that accepts `CancellationToken`.
2. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` grace period** — replaced by `Join(timeout)` which actually waits for the thread rather than sleeping blindly.
3. **`ResetAbort` + continue pattern** — in cooperative cancellation, you simply check `IsCancellationRequested` and decide whether to continue or exit.
4. **Cleanup in catch** — `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` moves to the `OperationCanceledException` catch; `FlushBuffers()` stays in `finally`.
5. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — it's `IDisposable`; leaking it causes timer/callback leaks.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (moved from ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just exit the loop naturally
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        if (_workerThread.IsAlive)
            _workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after timeout, the thread is truly stuck in
        // uncooperative code. Log a warning — there is no safe forced
        // termination in modern .NET.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### What changed in `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue or I/O), it **must** accept and honor the token — otherwise cancellation stalls until it returns on its own:

```csharp
// Before (.NET Framework) — not cancellable
private WorkItem GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // blocks indefinitely
}

// After (.NET 8) — cancellable
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}
```

> **If you can't modify `GetNextItem`** (e.g., third-party library with no token overload), move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` as a last resort. There is no safe in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.
